## Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation Migration

### Pattern Classification

Your code has **three** patterns from the migration guide:

| Location | Pattern | Issue on .NET 8 |
|----------|---------|-----------------|
| `Stop()` → `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | ThreadAbortException as control flow | Never thrown — cleanup silently skipped |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | ResetAbort to continue execution | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

### Key Pitfalls in This Code

1. **`GetNextItem()` may block** — a `volatile bool` flag won't interrupt it. You need `CancellationToken` propagated into the blocking call.
2. **Cleanup logic in the catch block** (`RollbackCurrentTransaction`) will **never execute** on .NET 8 because `ThreadAbortException` is never thrown. This is a silent data-integrity bug.
3. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)`** is a fire-and-hope pattern — use `Thread.Join(timeout)` for deterministic waiting.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            bool itemStarted = false;
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                itemStarted = true;
                ProcessItem(item, token);      // pass token for checkpoints
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — roll back any partial work
                if (itemStarted)
                    RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit the loop cleanly
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel();  // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for the thread to finish
        if (_workerThread?.IsAlive == true)
            _workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

### What Changed and Why

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationToken` | Token integrates with blocking APIs; a bool can't wake a blocked thread |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Cooperative signal instead of preemptive kill |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` | The `when` filter avoids catching unrelated OCEs from other sources |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed — just `break` | No abort to reset; you decide whether to continue or exit |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` | `_workerThread.Join(2s)` | Deterministic wait that returns as soon as the thread exits |
| — | `IDisposable` | `CancellationTokenSource` owns timers/callbacks and must be disposed |

### Critical: Update Your Blocking Call

`GetNextItem()` **must** accept the token, or cancellation stalls there:

```csharp
// If it wraps a BlockingCollection<T>:
var item = _queue.Take(token);           // throws OCE on cancel

// If it wraps a WaitHandle:
WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _dataReady, token.WaitHandle });

// If it uses Thread.Sleep to poll:
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(pollInterval);  // replaces Thread.Sleep
```

If `GetNextItem` calls third-party code you **cannot modify** to accept a token, wrap it in a child process and use `Process.Kill` as the last resort — there is no in-process equivalent of `Thread.Abort` in modern .NET.
